Following are classes and interfaces in each packages.
1. package interfaces
    * interface Freezable                       --handles freeze and unfreeze for an account.
    * interface Transactable                    --makes transactions.
    * interface AccountViewer                   --holds Account Number, balance, AccountType, frozen status and Transaction List and implements the abstract methods of Transactable, Freezable interfaces.

2. package bankexceptions
    * class AccountNotFoundException            --for Account is not present
    * class AccountFrozenException              --for Transaction made in Frozen Account
    * class CustomerNotFoundException           --for Customer is not Found
    * class InsufficientFundsException          --if user wants to withdraw beyond the balance
    * class InvalidAmountException              --if user enters invalid value for withdraw or deposit amount (say, negative number).

    -- All the above class extends the Exception class.

3. package account
    * abstract class Account  --Abstract class that defines common fields (accountNumber, balance, customer, transactionList, frozen status) and abstract methods for deposit, withdraw, transfer.
    * class CurrentAccount    --Current account without interest calculation
    * class SavingsAccount    --Savings account with interest calculation
    * class Transaction       --To hold the details of each transaction. 

4. package User
    * class Customer          --To hold customer details like name, age, address, phone number and list of accounts.
    * class CustomerConsole   --Runs the console for Customer dashboard

5. package bank
    * class Clerk             --For one who creates a customer.
    * class Manager           --For one who creates an account for a customer.
    * class Branch            --To hold branch details like branch name, branch code, manager, clerk and list of customers.
    * class Bank              --To hold bank details like bank name, IFSC code and list of branches.
    
 6. package database
    *class DataStorage        --uses methods available in FileHandling class such as, saveTofile for loading the account, customer and transaction data from console to file and loadFromfile to readback from file and store it to Collections like list for searching, modification and creation.
    *class FileHandling       --Load and Store data to file using Input/OutputStreams 


        1. Classes & Objects
    •	Meaningful Classes:
    Customer, Account (abstract), SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, Transaction, Bank, Branch, Clerk, Manager.
    •	Attributes & Behaviors:
    Example: Customer has fields like customerId, name and methods like openAccount(), viewAccounts().
    •	Objects in Main:
    Objects are created and managed in BankingSystem and console classes.
    2. Encapsulation
    •	Private Fields:
    All entity classes use private fields, e.g., private double balance; in Account.
    •	Getters/Setters:
    Public getter/setter methods control access.
    3. Inheritance
    •	Base & Derived Classes:
    Account is the base class; SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount extend it.
    •	Method Overriding:
    withdraw() and deposit() are overridden in subclasses.
    •	super Keyword:
    Used in subclass constructors and overridden methods to call parent logic.
    4. Polymorphism
    •	Compile-time (Overloading):
    Constructors and methods like deposit(double) and deposit(double, String) 
    •	Runtime (Overriding):
    withdraw() and deposit() methods are overridden.
    •	Dynamic Dispatch:
    Account reference points to SavingsAccount or CurrentAccount objects.
    5. Abstraction
    •	Abstract Class:
    Account is abstract with abstract methods.
    •	Interfaces:
    Freezable, Transactable, AccountViewer interfaces are implemented by relevant classes.
    •	Hiding Details:
    Only essential behaviors are exposed; implementation details are hidden.
    6. Constructors
    •	Default & Parameterized:
    Classes like Customer and Account have both types.
    •	Overloading:
    Multiple constructors with different parameters.
    •	Chaining:
    Use of this() and super() in constructors.
    7. Access Modifiers
    •	All Four Used:
    private for fields, public for APIs, protected for inheritance, default for package-private members.
    •	Visibility:
    Demonstrated across classes and packages.
    8. Packages
    •	User-defined Packages:
    Examples: accounts, user, bank, database, interfaces, bankexceptions.
    •	Import & Use:
    Classes are imported and used across packages.
    9. Exception Handling
    •	try-catch-finally:
    Used in transaction and file operations.
    •	Custom Exceptions:
    AccountNotFoundException, InsufficientFundsException,  are thrown and caught.
    10. Generics
    •	Bounded Generics:
    Example: <T extends Account>.
    11.Collections Framework
    •	List:
    ArrayList<Account> for storing accounts.
    •	Sorting/Filtering:
    Sorting accounts by balance, filtering with lambda expressions.
    12. File Handling
    •	Read/Write:
    Customer and account data are loaded from and saved to CSV files using DataStorage and FileHandler.
    